\section{The client}

The client is shaped like Unpaywall's: an HTTP session, a per-DOI cache
in its own [[europepmc.pkl]] (so [[scholar cache info]] and [[scholar
cache clear --provider europepmc]] cover it), and a limiter declared as
[[LIMITS]], which [[scholar providers limits]] lists
(\cref{sec:ratelimit-registry}).
It keeps the outcome of its last lookup the way the providers' DOI
lookups do (\cref{sec:lookup-outcome}): [[last_lookup_error]] when the
lookup failed, [[last_lookup_declined]] when there was nothing it could
ask.

<<the europe pmc client>>=
class EuropePMCClient:
    """Looks up abstracts for DOIs via Europe PMC's RESTful search."""

    name = "europepmc"
    LIMITS = Limits(per_second=EUROPEPMC_REQUESTS_PER_SECOND)

    def __init__(self) -> None:
        self._cache: dict = load_cache(self.name)
        register_cache(self.name, self._cache)
        self._limiter = limiter_for(self)
        self._session = self._create_session()
        self.last_lookup_error: str | None = None
        self.last_lookup_declined: str | None = None

    <<europe pmc client methods>>
<<constants>>=
EUROPEPMC_SEARCH_URL = (
    "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search"
)
EUROPEPMC_TIMEOUT = 30
EUROPEPMC_REQUESTS_PER_SECOND = 5.0  # scholar's choice; see the prose
@

The session is a [[ScholarSession]] (\cref{ch:network}), with
scholar's User-Agent and the configured route, and the back-off the
other clients use for a 5xx, so a transient server error is retried
before it counts as a failure.
A 429 is deliberately not retried by the session, for the reason given
for Crossref's (\cref{crossref-module}): [[urllib3]] would retry it out
of the limiter's sight and end in [[RetryError]], so no cooldown would
begin and every following paper would repeat the burst.
Left to [[record_response]], one 429 starts a cooldown, and the
following lookups are refused by the limiter without a request and
counted as failed in the enrichment tally.

<<europe pmc client methods>>=
def _create_session(self) -> requests.Session:
    """Create a session that retries a 5xx; a 429 goes to the limiter."""
    retry = Retry(
        total=3,
        backoff_factor=1,  # 1s, 2s, 4s delays
        status_forcelist=[500, 502, 503, 504],
        allowed_methods=["GET"],
    )
    return network.new_session(retry)
@

\subsection{Looking an abstract up}

The lookup returns the abstract, or [[None]] for every other outcome,
and leaves in the attributes which outcome it was.
An answer---one record with an abstract, records without one, or no
record---is cached, the absence of an abstract as [[None]], so a DOI
Europe PMC does not hold is not asked again on the next run.
A failure is not cached, and [[last_lookup_error]] holds the reason in
the words of [[network.request_failure_reason]]
(\cref{sec:request-failure-reason}).
An answer that is JSON but has no result list is a failure too: we
cannot tell it from an answer, so it must not be cached as one.
The cache holds the abstract as the service sent it, and it is read
into plain text when it is returned, as Crossref's is; a different
reading then needs no new request.

A DOI holding a double quote cannot be put inside the speech marks of a
phrase, and the OpenAPI description says nothing about escaping one; we
decline such a DOI instead of sending a query whose meaning we do not
know.

Several results are asked for ([[EUROPEPMC_PAGE_SIZE]]) because we take
the first whose DOI is ours and which has an abstract, and the
documentation does not promise that a DOI matches one record only.

<<constants>>=
EUROPEPMC_PAGE_SIZE = 5
<<europe pmc client methods>>=
def fetch_abstract(self, doi: str | None) -> str | None:
    """Return the abstract Europe PMC holds for `doi`, or None.

    None when there is no DOI, Europe PMC holds no record with an
    abstract (with text) for it, the DOI cannot be asked for, or the lookup
    failed; the last two set `last_lookup_declined` and
    `last_lookup_error`.
    """
    self.last_lookup_error = None
    self.last_lookup_declined = None
    if not doi:
        return None
    if '"' in doi:
        self.last_lookup_declined = EUROPEPMC_UNQUOTABLE_DOI
        return None
    key = ("abstract", doi)
    if key in self._cache:
        logger.debug("europepmc: cache hit for DOI %s", doi)
        return abstract_text(self._cache[key])
    try:
        self._limiter.acquire()
        response = self._session.get(
            EUROPEPMC_SEARCH_URL,
            params={
                "query": f'DOI:"{doi}"',
                "resultType": "core",
                "format": "json",
                "pageSize": EUROPEPMC_PAGE_SIZE,
            },
            timeout=EUROPEPMC_TIMEOUT,
        )
        self._limiter.record_response(response)
        response.raise_for_status()
        abstract = abstract_for_doi(doi, response.json())
    except RateLimited as e:
        self.last_lookup_error = e.reason
        return None
    except Exception as e:
        logger.warning(
            "europepmc: lookup failed for %s: %s",
            doi, network.redact_secrets(e),
        )
        self.last_lookup_error = network.request_failure_reason(e)
        return None
    self._cache[key] = abstract
    return abstract_text(abstract)
<<constants>>=
EUROPEPMC_UNQUOTABLE_DOI = "the DOI holds a double quote"
@

Reading the answer is a function of its own, so the tests can feed it
the recorded bodies directly.
The recorded abstract is plain text; what markup other records may
carry is not documented in what we could read.
So the text is read with [[markup_to_text]], the reading Crossref's
JATS abstracts get (\cref{sec:markup-to-text}): HTML headings,
paragraphs and line breaks become spaces, inline tags vanish, entities
are decoded; the limits of reading plain text that way are stated
there.
[[clean_metadata_text]] alone would not do: it removes a fixed list of
inline tags and keeps [[<h4>]], [[<p>]] and [[<br/>]].
A record whose abstract leaves no text is passed over for the next
record under the same DOI, and a lookup that finds no text at all
returns [[None]], never an empty string.

<<module-level europe pmc interface>>=
def abstract_text(raw: str | None) -> str | None:
    """Return the plain text of an abstract as sent, or None if empty."""
    return (markup_to_text(raw) or None) if raw else None


def abstract_for_doi(doi: str, answer: dict) -> str | None:
    """Return the abstract, as sent, of the first result under `doi`.

    Results whose abstract is missing or leaves no text are passed
    over. `answer` is a decoded search response. Raises `ValueError`
    when it has no result list, since that cannot be told from "no
    record".
    """
    try:
        results = answer["resultList"]["result"]
    except (KeyError, TypeError):
        raise ValueError("answer without a result list") from None
    wanted = doi.casefold()
    for record in results or []:
        raw = record.get("abstractText")
        if (
            str(record.get("doi") or "").casefold() == wanted
            and isinstance(raw, str)
            and abstract_text(raw)
        ):
            return raw
    return None
@

Enrichment asks for a paper ([[get_paper_by_doi]],
\cref{enrich-module}); ours holds the DOI and the abstract only, with
Europe PMC as its source, as Crossref's does
(\cref{sec:crossref-abstract}).

<<europe pmc client methods>>=
def get_paper_by_doi(self, doi: str | None) -> Paper | None:
    """Return a paper holding only `doi` and its Europe PMC abstract."""
    abstract = self.fetch_abstract(doi)
    if not abstract:
        return None
    return Paper(
        title="", authors=[], doi=doi, abstract=abstract,
        sources=[self.name],
    )
@

\section{The module-level interface}

One client is built at import, so its cache, session and limiter are
shared by the whole process; enrichment asks that client.

<<module-level europe pmc interface>>=
_client = EuropePMCClient()


def fetch_abstract(doi: str | None) -> str | None:
    """Look up the abstract of a DOI (see EuropePMCClient)."""
    return _client.fetch_abstract(doi)


def last_lookup_error() -> str | None:
    """Why the shared client's last lookup failed; None if it did not."""
    return _client.last_lookup_error


def enrichment_source() -> EuropePMCClient:
    """The shared client, as an abstract source for `scholar.enrich`."""
    return _client
